{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Class\n\n`src/rebar/_store/lock.py` — **384 `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'flock'`\nplus 4 `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'fcntl'`** in job `100874330553`, roughly 74% of the\n510 failures and the single largest class by an order of magnitude.\n\n`lock.py:72-75` guards the **import**:\n\n```python\ntry:  # POSIX advisory locking; absent on some platforms (e.g. plain Windows)\n    import fcntl\nexcept ImportError:\n    fcntl = None\n```\n\nbut there is no `is not None` check at either **use** site — `lock.py:207` in `_acquire_fcntl` and\n`lock.py:254` in `write_lock_is_busy`. Every store write reaches `_acquire_once` → `_acquire_fcntl`\n(`lock.py:491`), so on Windows every ticket write raises.\n\nTwo sibling modules already gate their **use** sites with `if fcntl is not None:` —\n`_snapshot/git_fetch.py:79` and `_snapshot/janitor.py:430` — which makes `lock.py` an **outlier\nrather than a deliberate choice**. (`reducer/marker.py` is NOT such a sibling: it guards the\nimport but calls `fcntl.flock` unguarded at :33/:37, so it shares this defect. That is tracked\nseparately as bug `nitro-heavy-bluefish` and is explicitly OUT of S1's scope — it is the\nper-ticket archive-marker lock, not the store write lock in this story's file impact.)\n\n## THE OBVIOUS TWO-LINE FIX IS UNSAFE. DO NOT TAKE IT.\n\nSkipping the fcntl leg when `fcntl is None` looks like two lines. It silently breaks a documented,\nload-bearing invariant.\n\n`_acquire_mkdir` (`lock.py:325-362`) passes `fcntl_held=True` into stale-lock reclamation, and its\nown docstring states the precondition verbatim:\n\n> **Precondition (load bearing, hence `fcntl_held=True` below):** this is only ever reached from\n> `acquire` AFTER the exclusive `fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX)` leg on the same tracker's\n> `.ticket-write.lock` has been taken … That makes reclamation race-safe … if that owner were still\n> alive it would still hold this fcntl lock and we could not be here.\n\nDegrade to mkdir-only and `fcntl_held=True` becomes **a lie**. rebar would reclaim a write lock held\nby a **live** process. That is silent store corruption under concurrency — strictly worse than not\nsupporting Windows at all, because the failure is invisible instead of loud.\n\n## What a correct fix requires\n\n1. **A real alternative exclusive leg** on Windows — `msvcrt.locking(fd, LK_NBLCK, …)` or\n   `LockFileEx` via `ctypes`/`pywin32` — that provides the same *kernel-mediated, dies-with-the-\n   process* exclusivity `flock` provides. A userspace substitute that does not die with its holder\n   does NOT satisfy the precondition.\n2. **A reworked staleness proof.** The current proof leans on `flock` being kernel-mediated on the\n   inode and therefore shared across pid/mount namespaces on one kernel. The Windows leg's proof\n   must be stated explicitly in the docstring and must be its own argument, not a copy of the POSIX\n   one. If the Windows leg cannot support `fcntl_held=True`, the reclamation path must be narrowed\n   on Windows rather than the flag falsified.\n3. **A test that fails if the invariant is degraded** — the epic's AC5 and `f850`'s AC5, carried\n   forward unchanged. A change that makes `_acquire_mkdir` reachable without an exclusive leg held\n   must turn a test red.\n\n## This story GATES the rest of the epic\n\nUntil store writes work on Windows, the other classes cannot be measured: their tests fail during\nstore setup before reaching the semantics under test. Concretely, the bug's class list named\n\".EXE console-script invocation\" as an independent class, but the log shows those are\n`subprocess.CalledProcessError` on `…\\.venv\\Scripts\\rebar.EXE 'create' 'task'` whose captured\nstderr is this very `flock` `AttributeError` — downstream of S1, not separate. Expect the residual\nclass sizes to move once this lands.\n\n`S1 blocks` every other child of the epic.\n\n## Proving command\n\n```\nuv run --no-sync pytest tests/unit/test_store_write_lock.py tests/unit/test_lock_owner_staleness.py \\\n  tests/interfaces/store/test_mcp_write_lock_dead_stamp.py \\\n  tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/test_inbound_leaf_bodies.py \\\n  -p no:cacheprovider -q\n```\nrun on `windows-latest`, plus the unchanged POSIX run of the same selection on `ubuntu-latest` to\nprove no regression of the existing proof. (Adjust the first two paths to the actual lock test\nmodules present at implementation time; the third and fourth are named from the observed failure\ncensus — `test_mcp_write_lock_dead_stamp.py` is a collection ERROR today and\n`test_inbound_leaf_bodies.py` is the single largest failing file at 27.)\n\nWhole-class check afterwards:\n```\nuv run --no-sync pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -q -p no:cacheprovider 2>&1 \\\n  | grep -c \"has no attribute 'flock'\"   # must be 0\n```\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `fcntl` is never dereferenced when it is `None`; a Windows run reaches the same\n      exclusivity guarantee by a different documented mechanism. Checked by\n      `pytest tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py -q`, which drives the real\n      `acquire()` with the `fcntl` primitive stubbed absent.\n- [ ] `_acquire_mkdir`'s `fcntl_held=True` argument is still true under the Windows leg,\n      with the Windows proof (kernel-mediated file lock released on handle close, hence on\n      process death) written out in the docstring — or it is not passed and reclamation is\n      correspondingly narrowed. Checked by\n      `pytest tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py -k precondition -q`.\n- [ ] A test fails if the exclusivity precondition is degraded: with NO exclusive leg\n      available, `acquire()` raises and never creates `.ticket-write.lock.d`. Checked by\n      `pytest tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py -k no_leg -q`.\n- [ ] POSIX behaviour is unchanged: the existing concurrency gates stay green. Checked by\n      `pytest tests/integration/test_concurrency_regression.py tests/integration/test_store_concurrency.py tests/interfaces/store/test_reads_under_sync_contention.py -q`.\n- [ ] The `flock` `AttributeError` count in a Windows sweep is 0 (verified by the epic's\n      `sweep (windows)` job once this lands).\n\n## Testing\n\n1. RED first: `pytest tests/unit/store/test_windows_exclusive_leg.py -q` must fail before\n   the fix exists (the `fcntl`-absent path raises `AttributeError`/`NoneType`).\n2. Platform-seam simulation on this POSIX host: the exclusive-leg selector is driven with\n   the `fcntl` primitive stubbed absent and a Windows-shaped primitive supplied, so the\n   Windows branch of the real `acquire()` runs here rather than being asserted about.\n3. Mutation check: deleting the no-leg guard, or negating the leg-selection branch, must\n   turn at least one of the above tests RED.\n4. POSIX no-regression: `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the three concurrency suites\n   named in the Acceptance Criteria, run before and after the change."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788575526385816001,"uuid":"c377a01f-266a-4757-9c6c-0aa35d8106a0"}